NNAAMMEE
     ggppttzzffssbboooott — GPT bootcode for ZFS on BIOS-based computers

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     ggppttzzffssbboooott is used on BIOS-based computers to boot from a filesystem in a
     ZFS pool.  ggppttzzffssbboooott is installed in a ffrreeeebbssdd--bboooott partition of a GPT-
     partitioned disk with gpart(8).

IIMMPPLLEEMMEENNTTAATTIIOONN NNOOTTEESS
     The GPT standard allows a variable number of partitions, but ggppttzzffssbboooott
     only boots from tables with 128 partitions or less.

BBOOOOTTIINNGG
     ggppttzzffssbboooott tries to find all ZFS pools that are composed of BIOS-visible
     hard disks or partitions on them.  ggppttzzffssbboooott looks for ZFS device labels
     on all visible disks and in discovered supported partitions for all sup‐
     ported partition scheme types.  The search starts with the disk from
     which ggppttzzffssbboooott itself was loaded.  Other disks are probed in BIOS de‐
     fined order.  After a disk is probed and ggppttzzffssbboooott determines that the
     whole disk is not a ZFS pool member, the individual partitions are probed
     in their partition table order.  Currently GPT and MBR partition schemes
     are supported.  With the GPT scheme, only partitions of type ffrreeeebbssdd--zzffss
     are probed.  The first pool seen during probing is used as a default boot
     pool.

     The filesystem specified by the bboooottffss property of the pool is used as a
     default boot filesystem.  If the bboooottffss property is not set, then the
     root filesystem of the pool is used as the default.  loader(8) is loaded
     from the boot filesystem.  If _/_b_o_o_t_._c_o_n_f_i_g or _/_b_o_o_t_/_c_o_n_f_i_g is present in
     the boot filesystem, boot options are read from it in the same way as
     boot(8).

     The ZFS GUIDs of the first successfully probed device and the first de‐
     tected pool are made available to loader(8) in the
     vvffss..zzffss..bboooott..pprriimmaarryy__vvddeevv and vvffss..zzffss..bboooott..pprriimmaarryy__ppooooll variables.

UUSSAAGGEE
     Normally ggppttzzffssbboooott will boot in fully automatic mode.  However, like
     boot(8), it is possible to interrupt the automatic boot process and in‐
     teract with ggppttzzffssbboooott through a prompt.  ggppttzzffssbboooott accepts all the op‐
     tions that boot(8) supports.

     The filesystem specification and the path to loader(8) are different from
     boot(8).  The format is

     [zfs:pool/filesystem:][/path/to/loader]

     Both the filesystem and the path can be specified.  If only a path is
     specified, then the default filesystem is used.  If only a pool and
     filesystem are specified, then _/_b_o_o_t_/_l_o_a_d_e_r is used as a path.

     Additionally, the ssttaattuuss command can be used to query information about
     discovered pools.  The output format is similar to that of zzppooooll ssttaattuuss
     (see zpool(8)).

     The configured or automatically determined ZFS boot filesystem is stored
     in the loader(8) llooaaddddeevv variable, and also set as the initial value of
     the ccuurrrrddeevv variable.

FFIILLEESS
     /boot/gptzfsboot  boot code binary
     /boot.config      parameters for the boot block (optional)
     /boot/config      alternative parameters for the boot block (optional)

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     ggppttzzffssbboooott is typically installed in combination with a “protective MBR”
     (see gpart(8)).  To install ggppttzzffssbboooott on the _a_d_a_0 drive:

           gpart bootcode -b /boot/pmbr -p /boot/gptzfsboot -i 1 ada0

     ggppttzzffssbboooott can also be installed without the PMBR:

           gpart bootcode -p /boot/gptzfsboot -i 1 ada0

SSEEEE AALLSSOO
     boot.config(5), boot(8), gpart(8), loader(8), zpool(8)

HHIISSTTOORRYY
     ggppttzzffssbboooott appeared in FreeBSD 7.3.

AAUUTTHHOORRSS
     This manual page was written by Andriy Gapon <avg@FreeBSD.org>.

BBUUGGSS
     ggppttzzffssbboooott looks for ZFS meta-data only in MBR partitions (known on
     FreeBSD as slices).  It does not look into BSD disklabel(8) partitions
     that are traditionally called partitions.  If a disklabel partition hap‐
     pens to be placed so that ZFS meta-data can be found at the fixed offsets
     relative to a slice, then ggppttzzffssbboooott will recognize the partition as a
     part of a ZFS pool, but this is not guaranteed to happen.

